"Node is a runtime for running JavaScript outside the browser, built on Chrome's V8 engine. It is used for servers, APIs and tools like Vite. My code runs on a single thread, but slow operations, like reading a file or querying the database, do not block it: Node hands them off and keeps serving other requests. When they finish, the event loop runs their callback. That is why it is great for lots of I/O and not so great for heavy computation."

```js
import { readFile } from "node:fs/promises";

// No bloquea: mientras lee el archivo, Node atiende otras peticiones
const contenido = await readFile("datos.json", "utf8");
```

"It is a function that runs in the middle, between the request arriving and the response being sent. It receives the request, the response and `next`. It can inspect or modify the request, respond directly, or call `next` to move on. It is used for things many routes share: parsing the JSON body, logging, checking that the user is logged in, or enabling CORS."

```js
const app = express();
app.use(express.json()); // middleware: convierte el cuerpo en req.body

function requiereLogin(req, res, next) {
  if (!req.headers.authorization) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: "Falta el token" });
  }
  next(); // sigue hacia la ruta
}

app.get("/api/perfil", requiereLogin, (req, res) => res.json({ ok: true }));
```

"First I validate the input: if data is missing or malformed, I respond with a 400 and a clear message and stop there. For unexpected errors I have an error middleware at the end that catches them, logs the full details, and sends the client a 500 with a generic message, without the stack trace or internal data. That way the frontend always gets the same error format and knows what to show."

```js
app.post("/api/pedidos", async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    if (!req.body.productoId) {
      return res.status(400).json({ error: "productoId es obligatorio" });
    }
    const pedido = await crearPedido(req.body);
    res.status(201).json(pedido);
  } catch (error) {
    next(error); // lo manda al middleware de errores
  }
});

app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  console.error(err);
  res.status(500).json({ error: "Error interno, intenta más tarde" });
});
```

"The user sends their email and password, the server verifies them and returns a signed token. From then on, the frontend sends that token with every request, usually in the `Authorization` header. The server checks the signature and, if it is valid, knows who the user is without storing a session. A JWT has three parts: header, payload and signature. The payload can be read because it is not encrypted, so I never put sensitive data in it."

```js
// Frontend: enviar el token en cada petición
const respuesta = await fetch("/api/perfil", {
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
});
```

"It happens when I build a query by pasting user text directly into the SQL. If someone types something like `' OR '1'='1` into the login form, it changes the meaning of the query and they can get in without a password or even delete data. It is prevented with parameterized queries: I write the query with placeholders and pass the values separately, so the database always treats them as data, never as code. ORMs like Prisma do this for me."

```js
// Mal: el texto del usuario se vuelve parte del SQL
db.query(`SELECT * FROM usuarios WHERE email = '${email}'`);

// Bien: consulta parametrizada
db.query("SELECT * FROM usuarios WHERE email = $1", [email]);
```

"It is a group of operations that run as one: either all of them happen or none do. The classic example is a transfer: subtract from one account and add to another. If the second step fails, the first one is undone with a rollback and the money does not vanish. In a store I would use one to create an order and reduce the stock at the same time."

```sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE cuentas SET saldo = saldo - 100 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE cuentas SET saldo = saldo + 100 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT; -- si algo falla antes, se hace ROLLBACK y nada cambia
```
